series: a 1900 figure for "Austria-Hungary" is not a figure for present-day
Austria. This function estimates one by spatial reallocation (dasymetric
areal interpolation):
\enumerate{
\item For each data \code{year}, the value reported by each source polity is spread
over \strong{that polity's own extent for that year} across a regular grid,
weighted by a \code{covariate} density (e.g. gridded cropland or population;
uniform = plain areal weighting).
\item The grid is then re-aggregated to the \code{ref_year} target boundaries: a
target's estimate is the sum of grid mass falling inside it.
\item Target territory \strong{not covered} by any source with data in that year is
\emph{imputed} — its grid cells still carry covariate mass, so they are filled
at a donor intensity (value per unit covariate) rather than left at zero.
The fraction of a target's covariate mass that had to be imputed is
reported as \code{imputed_share}, an honest confidence signal.
